From 767b3d66c17a367eb6f2bc93e83e8d55285f07eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hizuru254 <2654837056@qq.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:56:27 +0800
Subject: [03/14] protocol v1.5/v1.6: anneal the interface and discard the flux
 transient

The TBR was not stationary because (a) the as-built interfaces keep relaxing at
~0.4-1.4 eV/ps at 300 K and (b) postprocess compares the two halves of a
production segment that still contains the 0-100 ps flux transient.

v1.5 adds a flux warm-up stage (__N_WARM__) that is excluded from the
measurement; v1.6 also anneals 300->700->300 K inside the same input so the
interface reaches its potential-energy plateau before the flux is applied.

Results (flux 1 eV/ps, 305k steps, 24 ranks, 41 min per point):
  run_v038 flat : TBR 5.33e-9 +- 0.43, time conv 12.6 %, drift +0.87 K
  run_v039 P350 : TBR 4.69e-9 +- 0.47, time conv 31.7 %, drift +0.99 K
Temperature stability, energy conservation (1.8e-7) and flux symmetry (1.5e-5)
are now all well inside the gates. The remaining 12.6 % half-to-half spread of
the flat case is 1.7 sigma of its own statistical error, i.e. no resolvable
drift; reaching 5 % requires ~10x more averaging (1.5-3 ns per point).

With the interface properly equilibrated the pattern advantage disappears:
P350/flat goes from 0.60x (60 ps screening, 2.2 sigma) to 0.88x (1 sigma).
The earlier 'best pattern' claim is therefore withdrawn for the second time,
now on the grounds that the effect is smaller than the measurement error.

R^2 >= 0.995 remains structurally unreachable for this stack (docs/PROTOCOL_V13.md R4).
